---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
---

<Layout title="Cardboard Castle — Christopher St George">
  <article class="project-page">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Back to all projects</a>

    <h1>Cardboard Castle</h1>
    <p class="project-meta">
      Environment Artist &amp; Puzzle Designer
    </p>

    <div class="project-body">
      <div class="project-text">
        <section>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
          <p>
            Cardboard Castle is a Unity VR puzzle game that me and a team of 3
            other students developed for QUT's Design and Development of
            Immersive Environments unit. We were tasked with creating a VR game
            under the theme 'Scale is a Lie' — from this simple theme, we
            developed the central mechanic: a magical wand that could grow and
            shrink objects. Players could use this wand to solve various puzzles
            across 5 different levels, starting from the depths of the castle
            dungeon and eventually making their way to the gatehouse to escape.
          </p>
          <p>
            The game was intended to be played at VR festivals/showcases (which
            did end up happening when we showed it off at the 2026 QUT open
            day!), so our target audience were VR beginners, with a specific
            focus on under 14s. Because of this, I went for an eclectic,
            hand-drawn aesthetic that drew a lot of inspiration from the Little
            Big Planet games and their 'toybox' feel.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>What I did</h2>
          <p>
            Cardboard Castle was my first time ever making a fully custom indoor
            environment and, while the models were pretty simple, I ran into some
            challenges with creating texture variants that fit seamlessly in
            different contexts. I also had to keep performance constraints in
            mind — because we wanted a game that ran natively on the headset,
            texture size and count had to be limited in order to mitigate
            filesize. All of the wall, floor, and roof pieces were atlased in a
            single texture, which was another first for me as I had never done
            texture atlasing prior to this!
          </p>
          <p>
            This project was probably one of the most valuable learning
            experiences for me in terms of hard skills. I learnt a lot of
            important optimisation lessons that have carried over into pretty
            much all my other development projects. Special shoutout to Mark
            Auman for the lecture he gave on optimisation during this unit, which
            was a massive help.
          </p>
          <p>
            In addition to modelling and texturing the environment, I also
            contributed the following:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>All puzzle designs</li>
            <li>NPC model and texture</li>
            <li>Some prop models and textures</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>Tools &amp; technologies</h2>
          <ul class="tag-list">
            <li>Substance Painter</li>
            <li>Blender</li>
            <li>Unity</li>
            <li>Meta Development Kit</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="project-gallery">
        <!--
          EDIT THIS: once you have real images, put them in public/images/
          and change each "src" below from the placeholder URL to
          something like "/images/project-2-screenshot-1.jpg". Add or
          remove objects from this list for more or fewer photos.
        -->
        <Gallery
          images={[
            { src: 'https://placehold.co/800x600/1b1e25/9299a6?text=Environment+1', alt: 'Environment screenshot' },
            { src: 'https://placehold.co/800x600/1b1e25/9299a6?text=Props', alt: 'Modular props kit' },
            { src: 'https://placehold.co/800x600/1b1e25/9299a6?text=Environment+2', alt: 'Environment screenshot' },
          ]}
        />

        <!--
          EDIT THIS: paste your YouTube video's ID below — it's the part
          after "v=" in a normal youtube.com/watch?v=... URL. Then delete
          the placeholder <div> underneath and uncomment this iframe.

          <div class="project-video">
            <iframe
              src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/YOUTUBE_VIDEO_ID"
              title="Project video"
              allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
              allowfullscreen
            ></iframe>
          </div>
        -->
        <div class="video-placeholder">YouTube video will go here</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
